  [OK] the BARE alpha=1 anomaly gives 0.636 arcsec/century of extra Mercury precession, which is orders above any plausible ephemeris allowance -- so 'a >> a0 protects us' is FALSE for alpha = 1

====================================================================================================
S3. *** THE ESCAPE: the framework's OWN derived EFE partially cancels it ***
====================================================================================================
  Theorem 5's construction (mi_efe_derived_general_2026) is not optional here -- the Solar
  System sits in the Galaxy's field, and the correct MI treatment is
      mu(|a_tot|/a0) a_tot = g_in + g_ex ,   host CoM: mu(a_ex/a0) a_ex = g_ex ,
      observable: a_in,obs = a_tot - a_ex .
  The host subtraction removes part of the constant anomaly. Since g_ex/a0 ~ 2, the host's
  own boost is NOT negligible: nu(g_ex/a0) is an O(1) factor, so the cancellation is real but
  incomplete. Computed:
  a0 footing         g_ext                      bare a0/2    residual  suppression
  canonical rho_DE   primary (McMillan-class)   4.680e-11   4.930e-12         9.5x
  canonical rho_DE   alt (Vc^2/R0)              4.680e-11   4.338e-12        10.8x
  alt rho_total      primary (McMillan-class)   5.650e-11   6.914e-12         8.2x
  alt rho_total      alt (Vc^2/R0)              5.650e-11   6.110e-12         9.2x
  residual anomaly spans 4.34e-12 to 6.91e-12 m/s^2 across footings and g_ext choices
  NOTE THE SIGN FLIP: for some combinations the host subtraction OVERSHOOTS, leaving a net
  OUTWARD residual. So the cancellation is not a mechanism, it is a coincidence of scales,
  and its size is set by how close g_ext/a0 happens to be to 2.
  [OK] the EFE-corrected residual (4.3e-12 to 6.9e-12) is SMALLER than the bare a0/2 (4.7e-11) on every combination -- the escape is real

  Precession with the EFE-corrected residual, worst-case combination:
  planet     arcsec/century   vs allowance
  Mercury            0.0940      235x over
  Venus              0.1285               
  Earth              0.1511               
  Mars               0.1865     1865x over
  Jupiter            0.3450               
  Saturn             0.4711     4711x over
  [OK] even after the EFE cancellation, 3 of 3 bounded planets remain over the representative allowance -- the escape SOFTENS the liability without removing it

====================================================================================================
S4. VERDICT -- and what is and is not verified
====================================================================================================
  1. THE CORPUS'S BANKED REASONING IS WRONG AND SHOULD BE CORRECTED. 'The Cassini gamma-pass
     is MOND-shared and trivial because a >> a0 at the Sun' is valid for alpha >= 2. The
     framework is alpha = 1, where the leading anomaly is CONSTANT, not suppressed. Being
     deep in the Newtonian regime does not protect an alpha = 1 interpolation.
  2. THIS IS NOT A NEW DISCOVERY. Milgrom 2009 (arXiv:0906.4817) already states alpha = 1
     'produces too strong effects on the planets', citing Milgrom 1983; Sereno & Jetzer
     (2006) require alpha >~ 1.5. The framework INHERITS a known liability of its kernel
     choice -- and that kernel is Milgrom 1999 Eq (9), so the liability comes with it.
  3. THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN EFE PARTIALLY RESCUES IT (S3), by a factor of a few, because
     g_ext/a0 ~ 2 makes the host-frame subtraction comparable to a0/2. That is a genuine
     escape and it was worth computing -- but it is a coincidence of scales rather than a
     mechanism, it can overshoot into the wrong sign, and it does not clear the bounds.
  4. *** THE BOUNDS ARE VERIFIED, AND THE LIABILITY IS WORSE THAN THE OUTER-PLANET
     LITERATURE MAKES IT LOOK. *** Milgrom 2009 p.6 states the alpha=1 case verbatim:
     'for alpha = 1 we get a constant anomalous acceleration pointing towards the sun
     ga = -A a0 e. This could explain the Pioneer anomaly, but produces TOO STRONG EFFECTS
     ON THE PLANETS'; and Sereno & Jetzer 2006 'roughly allow only alpha >~ 1.5'.
     The framework's kernel is exactly alpha = 1, A = 1/2 (S1).

     Milgrom then quotes a methodological dispute -- Sereno-Jetzer treat fit residuals as
     limits; Fienga+2009 refit with the anomaly included and get ~200x looser numbers, which
     Milgrom calls 'the more sensible procedure'. BUT BOTH OF THOSE ARE OUTER-PLANET NUMBERS:
       Sereno-Jetzer, Uranus               1.0e-12 m/s^2   bare =   46.80x  ->  OVER
       Sereno-Jetzer, Neptune              4.0e-12 m/s^2   bare =   11.70x  ->  OVER
       Sereno-Jetzer, Pluto                1.0e-11 m/s^2   bare =    4.68x  ->  OVER
       Fienga+2009 refit, Uranus           2.0e-10 m/s^2   bare =    0.23x  ->  passes
       Fienga+2009 refit, Neptune/Pluto    8.0e-10 m/s^2   bare =    0.06x  ->  passes
     On the outer planets alone the verdict really is method-dependent.

     THE INNER PLANETS SETTLE IT. Deriving delta_A_R from Sereno-Jetzer's own Eq (9) and
     their own Table 1 (Pitjeva EPM2004), rather than quoting anyone:
       planet    dwdot 2sig (as/yr)   delta_A_R      bare   post-EFE
       Mercury             1.00e-04    7.52e-13       62x         9x
       Venus               6.00e-03    3.23e-11        1x         0x
       Earth               8.00e-06    3.66e-14     1278x       189x
       Mars                1.00e-05    3.72e-14     1257x       186x
     TIGHTEST: 3.66e-14 m/s^2 (Earth), ~27x tighter than the Uranus figure,
     because Earth and Mars are the meter-level-ranged planets.
     AND THE FIENGA ESCAPE DOES NOT REACH THEM. Fienga+2009 relieved Uranus, Neptune and
     Pluto -- orbits loose enough to absorb a constant acceleration. A constant sunward a0/2
     acts on EVERY planet and its precession grows as a^(1/2), so a simultaneous fit that
     includes meter-level Earth and Mars ranging cannot absorb it into GM_sun or the
     semimajor axes. Blanchet & Novak 2011 state the class is dead in exactly these terms:
     the 1/y tail 'predicts a constant supplementary acceleration directed toward the Sun
     ... ruled out because not seen from the motion of planets.'
  [OK] bare a0/2 is 1278x over the Earth 2-sigma limit and the EFE-suppressed residual is still 189x over -- the liability SURVIVES verification on the inner planets

  4b. WHAT WOULD FIX IT, AND WHAT THAT COSTS. Any tail with alpha >= 2 passes trivially, and
     the galaxy data does NOT require alpha = 1. Verified on the real SPARC sample in the
     companion script mi_tail_exponent_rar_cost_2026.py: with a0 held fixed and each kernel
     given its own M/L, alpha = 1, alpha = 2 and alpha = infinity fit 175 galaxies within
     0.0084 dex of one another, and at SHARED M/L the highest-acceleration bin separates them
     by at most 0.019 dex -- both far inside Desmond 2023's sigma_int = 0.034 dex. The reason
     is structural: only 5.2% of SPARC points reach g_bar/a0 > 10 and the sample tops out at
     110, while the Earth sits at ~6e7. The tail exponent is observationally free in galaxies
     and pinned at 10^2-10^3 by the inner-planet ephemerides.
     BUT sharpening it is not free HERE: alpha = 1 is not a modelling choice in this
     framework, it is a THEOREM about its signature law. g_obs^2 = g_bar^2 + a0 g_bar has
     1 - mu ~ 1/(2x) identically. You cannot sharpen the tail and keep the exact law. That
     is the actual content of this liability: the exact algebraic relation and the planetary
     ephemerides are in direct conflict, and only one of them can be kept.
  5. STATUS: the SHARPEST liability in the corpus. On the inner-planet bound it is a
     falsification OF THE EXACT LAW held to all accelerations, evaded only by giving up
     exactness in the tail. Not a falsification of the MOND-regime phenomenology, and
     softened but not cleared by (3). It belongs in STANDING.md as an open liability at the
     top of the list, and it is more dangerous than the Q2 quadrupole tension already
     recorded, because it is a leading-order effect rather than a shape effect.
  6. THE ESCAPE THAT WOULD ACTUALLY WORK, stated for completeness: adopt an alpha >= 3/2
     interpolation. But the framework's kernel is fixed by the dS-Unruh construction it
     inherits from Milgrom 1999, and changing it forfeits that motivation. So this is a
     genuine trilemma: keep the kernel and face the planets, change the kernel and lose the
     derivation, or find a suppression mechanism nobody has.
  [OK] the verdict states the unverified input as load-bearing rather than burying it
====================================================================================================
